module j3.net
    Non-blocking UDP and a byte packet reader/writer. No threads, no callbacks: poll `recv`
    once per tick until it returns `NONE`. The OS calls live in `j3.netsys` (Linux syscalls,
    macOS libc, Windows Winsock), picked by `[emit] target` the way `j3.sys` is.
    Packets are little-endian. Reliability, ordering, and connections are the game's job.
    Connections and the web are the other `j3.net` modules: `j3.net.tcp`, `j3.net.dns`,
    `j3.net.tls`, and `j3.net.http` (`j3c doc recipes http`).

const NONE: int = 0 - 1
    `recv` result: no datagram is waiting. Same value as `netsys.NONE` (a const cannot alias one).

const FAIL: int = 0 - 2
    `recv` / `send` result: the socket call failed. Same value as `netsys.FAIL`.

enum NetError
    Why `open` failed.
    Socket
        `socket` failed.
    Bind
        The port is taken or not allowed.

struct Addr
    An IPv4 address and port. `ip` is `a*2^24 + b*2^16 + c*2^8 + d`.
    ip: int = 0
        Four octets, first octet most significant.
    port: int = 0
        Host-order port.

fn addr(a: int, b: int, c: int, d: int, port: int): Addr
    `a.b.c.d:port`.
fn loopback(port: int): Addr
    `127.0.0.1:port`.
fn same(a: &Addr, b: &Addr): bool
    True when `a` and `b` name the same peer. Use it to map a datagram to a client slot.
fn open(port: int): Result<int, NetError>
    A UDP socket bound to `port` on every interface (`0` picks a free port), non-blocking.
    The `Ok` value is the handle; pass it to `send`, `recv`, `localPort`, `shut`.
fn localPort(fd: int): int
    The port `fd` is bound to. Use after `open(0)`; -1 on failure.
fn shut(fd: int)
    Close the socket.
fn send(fd: int, to: &Addr, data: []u8): int
    Send `data` as one datagram to `to`. Bytes sent, or `FAIL`. UDP drops are silent.
fn recv(fd: int, buf: []u8, from: &Addr): int
    Take one waiting datagram into `buf` and store its sender in `from`.
    Bytes read, `NONE` if nothing is waiting, or `FAIL`. A datagram longer than `buf` is cut.
    Call until `NONE` each tick, or packets pile up in the kernel.
fn localIPv4s(arena: &Arena): []string
    This machine's IPv4 addresses as `a.b.c.d` strings on `arena`, loopback skipped: what to show a
    phone that should connect to a `j3.net.tcp` listener. Linux and Windows; empty elsewhere.
struct Packet
    Cursor over a byte buffer, for writing a packet or reading one. Little-endian.
    Running past the end sets `bad` and writes or returns zeros, so a short or hostile
    packet is checked once at the end instead of after every field.
    bytes: []u8
        The whole buffer.
    pos: int = 0
        Next byte to write or read.
    bad: bool = false
        True once a put or get ran past the end.
    fn Packet.writer(buf: []u8): Packet
        Write into `buf` from the start.
    fn Packet.reader(data: []u8, n: int): Packet
        Read `data` (the first `n` bytes `recv` returned) from the start.
    fn Packet.done(this: &Packet): []u8
        What was written so far: hand this to `send`.
    fn Packet.left(this: &Packet): int
        Bytes left to read.
    fn Packet.room(this: &Packet, n: int): bool
        True if `n` more bytes fit; otherwise marks the packet bad.
    fn Packet.putU8(this: &Packet, v: int)
        Write one byte.
    fn Packet.putU16(this: &Packet, v: int)
        Write two bytes (0..65535).
    fn Packet.putU32(this: &Packet, v: int)
        Write four bytes (0..2^32-1, or any int: the low 32 bits).
    fn Packet.putI32(this: &Packet, v: int)
        Write a signed 32-bit int.
    fn Packet.putF32(this: &Packet, v: f32)
        Write an f32 as its four bytes.
    fn Packet.getU8(this: &Packet): int
        Read one byte, 0 when past the end.
    fn Packet.getU16(this: &Packet): int
        Read two bytes.
    fn Packet.getU32(this: &Packet): int
        Read four bytes as 0..2^32-1.
    fn Packet.getI32(this: &Packet): int
        Read a signed 32-bit int.
    fn Packet.getF32(this: &Packet): f32
        Read an f32, 0 when past the end.

